  "query_preview": "..\nMostly sunny skies across the forecast area early this \nafternoon as the mid/upper level high pressure resulting in extreme \nheat across the region is now located across east-central Arizona. \nThere is enough moisture under the high for some shower and \nthunderstorm activity across the White Mountains and these have been \ndeveloping in the past hour or two. Given lack of steering flow up \nthere, the bulk of the activity will remain over the higher terrain \nbut some outflows pushing southward will help result in some \nisolated storms later this afternoon and evening across Graham and \nCochise Counties. Main concern is gusty winds, though the strongest \ncores will have some locally heavy downpours. We also can't rule out \na stray storm or two along the Int'l border in Santa Cruz County \nlater this evening as the steering flow is a bit better along the \nInt'l border and can result in a few storms pushing in from adjacent \nCochise County. \n\nThe extreme heat will continue to be the main weather story this \nweekend and with Monday high temperatures now nudging slightly \nhigher, there is enough of our forecast area under major HeatRisk to \njustify an extension of the warning through Monday. \n\nIsolated to scattered thunderstorm coverage on Saturday \nafternoon/evening is expected east of Tucson with the best chances \ncloser to the AZ/NM border. SPC continues to highlight this area for \na marginal risk of severe thunderstorms due to the wind threat as \nthe 12Z HREF has greater than 10% 40-km neighborhood probabilities \nof wind gusts of 50 kts or greater.  \n\nA generally more active period for southeast Arizona may start as \nearly as Sunday, but much more likely by Monday and continuing \nthrough the rest of next week as a favorable easterly to \nnortheasterly pattern sets up with the strong high pressure \ngradually shifting into northern Arizona. With adequate moisture in \nour area and some embedded disturbances in the easterly flow (still \na bit hard to time this far out), some of these days look to be \nquite active. The general flavor will be more of a severe \nthunderstorm threat early in the week, transitioning to a hybrid \nsevere thunderstorm and flash flooding threat as the week \nprogresses. \n\nTemperatures from Tuesday onward lower to about 3 to 6 degrees above \nnormal.",
